{"in":{"id":19,"ok":{"global":{"path":"/tmp/mbe2e-C/data/agent/MEMORY.md","text":"# still.fail memory\n","sections":[],"size":"20 B"},"skills":[{"name":"stillfail-browser","description":"Use the station's own Chrome for web work that needs a login or should look like a person's browser (sites, dashboards, consoles, anything behind a sign-in), and ask a person to sign in or solve a captcha in it.","project":false,"builtin":true,"text":"---\nname: stillfail-browser\ndescription: Use the station's own Chrome for web work that needs a login or should look like a person's browser (sites, dashboards, consoles, anything behind a sign-in), and ask a person to sign in or solve a captcha in it.\n---\n\n# The station's browser\n\nEach station keeps one real Google Chrome with its own persistent profile: whatever someone signs into there stays\nsigned in, for every session on the station. Get it with the `browser` tool (it starts it if needed) and drive it\nwith your own client:\n\n- chrome-devtools-mcp: `npx -y chrome-devtools-mcp@latest --browserUrl http://127.0.0.1:<port>`\n- Playwright: `chromium.connectOverCDP(\"http://127.0.0.1:<port>\")`, then `browser.contexts()[0].newPage()` (the\n  default context has the sign-ins; a new context has none).\n\nRules:\n\n- Use it, not a Chrome or Chromium you launch yourself, for anything behind a login: a browser you start has none of\n  the sign-ins, and sites often refuse to sign in an automated one.\n- It is shared. Open a tab of your own and close it when done; leave other tabs alone; do not close the browser.\n- `browser` with action `tabs` lists the tabs; `stop` closes it for everyone (only when it is stuck).\n- It is closed after an hour unused and restarted if it dies while in use; call `browser` again when your\n  connection drops (the address usually stays).\n\n## Its tabs as screens (the `computer` tool)\n\nThe `computer` tool takes screenshots of a screen of the station and clicks, drags, scrolls, types and presses keys\non it, in the screenshot's pixels: a tab of this browser (`browser:<targetId>`, from `screen`) or the machine's display\n(`desktop`). For web pages prefer DevTools (Playwright, chrome-devtools-mcp): they read and act on the page itself and\nare faster and surer. Use `computer` for native apps on the desktop, and for what DevTools cannot reach (a browser\ndialog, a canvas). A person watching sees you work; while one is driving the screen your input is refused, so wait or\nask them. When you are stuck, hand over as below.\n\n## When a person is needed\n\nA sign-in (password, 2FA, a code sent to their phone), a captcha or a consent that only a person may give: do not try\nto get around it. Leave the page on that step, give the tab to a person with the `screen` tool (a link that opens the\ntab in their still.fail app, where they see it and type into it), ask in the chat with need_human (\"log in here\",\n\"solve the captcha\"), and end your turn. When they say it is done, carry on in the same tab: the sign-in is now in\nthe profile for later too.\n\nNever ask for passwords or codes in the chat, and never type someone's credentials you were not given for that\npurpose.\n","files":[],"share":null,"body":"# The station's browser\n\nEach station keeps one real Google Chrome with its own persistent profile: whatever someone signs into there stays\nsigned in, for every session on the station. Get it with the `browser` tool (it starts it if needed) and drive it\nwith your own client:\n\n- chrome-devtools-mcp: `npx -y chrome-devtools-mcp@latest --browserUrl http://127.0.0.1:<port>`\n- Playwright: `chromium.connectOverCDP(\"http://127.0.0.1:<port>\")`, then `browser.contexts()[0].newPage()` (the\n  default context has the sign-ins; a new context has none).\n\nRules:\n\n- Use it, not a Chrome or Chromium you launch yourself, for anything behind a login: a browser you start has none of\n  the sign-ins, and sites often refuse to sign in an automated one.\n- It is shared. Open a tab of your own and close it when done; leave other tabs alone; do not close the browser.\n- `browser` with action `tabs` lists the tabs; `stop` closes it for everyone (only when it is stuck).\n- It is closed after an hour unused and restarted if it dies while in use; call `browser` again when your\n  connection drops (the address usually stays).\n\n## Its tabs as screens (the `computer` tool)\n\nThe `computer` tool takes screenshots of a screen of the station and clicks, drags, scrolls, types and presses keys\non it, in the screenshot's pixels: a tab of this browser (`browser:<targetId>`, from `screen`) or the machine's display\n(`desktop`). For web pages prefer DevTools (Playwright, chrome-devtools-mcp): they read and act on the page itself and\nare faster and surer. Use `computer` for native apps on the desktop, and for what DevTools cannot reach (a browser\ndialog, a canvas). A person watching sees you work; while one is driving the screen your input is refused, so wait or\nask them. When you are stuck, hand over as below.\n\n## When a person is needed\n\nA sign-in (password, 2FA, a code sent to their phone), a captcha or a consent that only a person may give: do not try\nto get around it. Leave the page on that step, give the tab to a person with the `screen` tool (a link that opens the\ntab in their still.fail app, where they see it and type into it), ask in the chat with need_human (\"log in here\",\n\"solve the captcha\"), and end your turn. When they say it is done, carry on in the same tab: the sign-in is now in\nthe profile for later too.\n\nNever ask for passwords or codes in the chat, and never type someone's credentials you were not given for that\npurpose.","about":"Use the station's own Chrome for web work that needs a login or should look like a person's browser (sites, dashboards, consoles, anything behind a sign-in), and ask a person to sign in or solve a captcha in it.","brief":"Use the station's own Chrome for web work that needs a login or should look like a person's browser, and ask a person to sign in or solve a captcha in it.","size":"2.4 KB"},{"name":"stillfail-cron","description":"Run something on a schedule or at a set time (每天 9 点、每周一、每 30 分钟、明天提醒我, cron, a daily report, a reminder, a periodic check) with a still.fail job. Read it before setting up any scheduled or recurring task, instead of crontab, launchd, systemd timers or your runtime's own scheduler.","project":false,"builtin":true,"text":"---\nname: stillfail-cron\ndescription: Run something on a schedule or at a set time (每天 9 点、每周一、每 30 分钟、明天提醒我, cron, a daily report, a reminder, a periodic check) with a still.fail job. Read it before setting up any scheduled or recurring task, instead of crontab, launchd, systemd timers or your runtime's own scheduler.\n---\n\n# Scheduled tasks\n\nA schedule is one background job (stillfail-jobs) in the chat that asked for it: a shell loop that checks the clock and,\nwhen a run is due, either runs a command itself or wakes you with `stillfail-job notify` to do the work. Set it up in\none turn, without asking how: pick what the request leaves open and say it in the confirmation.\n\nDo not use crontab, launchd, systemd timers, Windows Task Scheduler, or your runtime's scheduler (Claude Code's\nCronCreate, /loop, ScheduleWakeup; Codex automations): nobody sees them in still.fail, they cannot wake you in this\nchat, they outlive or die apart from it, and a runtime's scheduler lives only as long as your process, which the\nstation ends when the chat is idle. A `waiting` turn's seconds are not a timer either.\n\n## 1. Write the loop\n\nWrite it to `cron/<name>.sh` in the session workspace (`<name>` short, e.g. `daily-report`). The loop compares numbers\nmade with `date` (same on macOS, Linux and Git's sh on Windows): no `date -d`/`date -j`, no `sleep` until a time.\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\n# 每天 09:00 (Asia/Shanghai): the daily report for #ops\nexport TZ=Asia/Shanghai\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\"\nlast=daily-report.last\nwhile :; do\n  slot=$(date +%F)                                   # this run's period: one a day\n  if [ \"$(date +%H%M)\" -ge 900 ] && [ \"$(cat \"$last\" 2>/dev/null)\" != \"$slot\" ]; then\n    echo \"$slot\" > \"$last\"\n    stillfail-job notify \"Scheduled run $slot of daily-report: <what to do, and where to post it>\"\n  fi\n  sleep 30\ndone\n```\n\nThe other schedules change only `slot` and the condition:\n\n| Schedule | `slot` | due when |\n|---|---|---|\n| every day at 09:00 | `$(date +%F)` | `[ \"$(date +%H%M)\" -ge 900 ]` |\n| weekdays at 18:30 | `$(date +%F)` | `[ \"$(date +%u)\" -le 5 ] && [ \"$(date +%H%M)\" -ge 1830 ]` |\n| Mondays at 10:00 | `$(date +%G-W%V)` | `[ \"$(date +%u)\" -eq 1 ] && [ \"$(date +%H%M)\" -ge 1000 ]` |\n| 1st of the month at 08:00 | `$(date +%Y-%m)` | `[ \"$(date +%d%H%M)\" -ge 10800 ]` |\n| every hour at :05 | `$(date +%F-%H)` | `[ \"$(date +%M)\" -ge 5 ]` |\n| every 30 minutes | `$(( $(date +%s) / 1800 ))` | always |\n| once, 2026-10-20 09:00 | `once` | `[ \"$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M)\" -ge 202610200900 ]`; then `echo` the instruction and `exit 0` instead of notifying: the job's end wakes you with its output |\n\nWrite numbers without leading zeros (`900`, not `0900`). The `.last` file is what makes it safe: the job is started\nagain after a station or machine restart and a run is never repeated, and one missed while the machine was off or\nasleep runs once when it is back within the same period (the same day for a daily one); older ones are not made up.\nCreated after today's time has passed, a daily schedule would run at once: when the person did not ask for a run now,\nwrite the current slot to `.last` before starting the job, so the first run is the next one.\n\nTime zone: the person's, set with `TZ` (a person writing Chinese is most likely on Asia/Shanghai; the station's own\nzone may differ). Name it in the confirmation so they can correct it.\n\n## 2. What a run does\n\n- **A command** (a backup, a sync, fetching a file): run it in the loop in place of the notify line, and tell only\n  what matters: `out=$(<command> 2>&1) || stillfail-job notify \"daily-backup $slot failed: $(printf '%s' \"$out\" | tail -5)\"`.\n  Nothing wakes you on success unless the person wants to hear of each run.\n- **Work for you** (a report, a summary, checking something and saying what changed, a reminder): the notify wakes\n  you. Make its words a complete instruction: the chat may have been compacted or be in a new session by then, so\n  name what to do, where to post (the thread address), and anything to compare against (a file in the workspace).\n\n## 3. Start it and confirm\n\n- `job_start` with `command: \"sh cron/<name>.sh\"`, `name` (e.g. `定时 · 每天 9:00 日报`) and `watch: true`: the chat is\n  then listed under 监控中, is never archived for being idle, and is asked about before archiving by hand (archiving\n  stops its jobs, and the schedule with them).\n- Post once in the chat: what runs, the schedule with its time zone, when the next run is, and that telling you in\n  this chat changes or stops it. Give the post a title naming the schedule (e.g. `定时 · 每天 9:00 日报`). End the\n  turn `waiting` (for: the schedule): with the watch running, only its notify, its end or a message wakes you.\n- On each run: do the work, post the result in the chat it is for, and end `waiting` again. A failed run: say so\n  there, fix the script if it is the cause, and restart the job.\n\n## Changing, stopping, several\n\n- `job_list` shows the schedules running; change one by editing its script, `job_stop` and `job_start` again (the\n  `.last` file carries over). Stop it with `job_stop` when asked, then end the chat normally.\n- Several schedules in one chat are several jobs (at most 20 jobs per chat).\n- A schedule runs on this station, and only while its machine is on: a laptop that sleeps runs a missed run when it\n  wakes. If it must run on time on another machine, the run can wake you and you use `station_task` there; or open the\n  chat on that station.\n","files":[],"share":null,"body":"# Scheduled tasks\n\nA schedule is one background job (stillfail-jobs) in the chat that asked for it: a shell loop that checks the clock and,\nwhen a run is due, either runs a command itself or wakes you with `stillfail-job notify` to do the work. Set it up in\none turn, without asking how: pick what the request leaves open and say it in the confirmation.\n\nDo not use crontab, launchd, systemd timers, Windows Task Scheduler, or your runtime's scheduler (Claude Code's\nCronCreate, /loop, ScheduleWakeup; Codex automations): nobody sees them in still.fail, they cannot wake you in this\nchat, they outlive or die apart from it, and a runtime's scheduler lives only as long as your process, which the\nstation ends when the chat is idle. A `waiting` turn's seconds are not a timer either.\n\n## 1. Write the loop\n\nWrite it to `cron/<name>.sh` in the session workspace (`<name>` short, e.g. `daily-report`). The loop compares numbers\nmade with `date` (same on macOS, Linux and Git's sh on Windows): no `date -d`/`date -j`, no `sleep` until a time.\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\n# 每天 09:00 (Asia/Shanghai): the daily report for #ops\nexport TZ=Asia/Shanghai\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\"\nlast=daily-report.last\nwhile :; do\n  slot=$(date +%F)                                   # this run's period: one a day\n  if [ \"$(date +%H%M)\" -ge 900 ] && [ \"$(cat \"$last\" 2>/dev/null)\" != \"$slot\" ]; then\n    echo \"$slot\" > \"$last\"\n    stillfail-job notify \"Scheduled run $slot of daily-report: <what to do, and where to post it>\"\n  fi\n  sleep 30\ndone\n```\n\nThe other schedules change only `slot` and the condition:\n\n| Schedule | `slot` | due when |\n|---|---|---|\n| every day at 09:00 | `$(date +%F)` | `[ \"$(date +%H%M)\" -ge 900 ]` |\n| weekdays at 18:30 | `$(date +%F)` | `[ \"$(date +%u)\" -le 5 ] && [ \"$(date +%H%M)\" -ge 1830 ]` |\n| Mondays at 10:00 | `$(date +%G-W%V)` | `[ \"$(date +%u)\" -eq 1 ] && [ \"$(date +%H%M)\" -ge 1000 ]` |\n| 1st of the month at 08:00 | `$(date +%Y-%m)` | `[ \"$(date +%d%H%M)\" -ge 10800 ]` |\n| every hour at :05 | `$(date +%F-%H)` | `[ \"$(date +%M)\" -ge 5 ]` |\n| every 30 minutes | `$(( $(date +%s) / 1800 ))` | always |\n| once, 2026-10-20 09:00 | `once` | `[ \"$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M)\" -ge 202610200900 ]`; then `echo` the instruction and `exit 0` instead of notifying: the job's end wakes you with its output |\n\nWrite numbers without leading zeros (`900`, not `0900`). The `.last` file is what makes it safe: the job is started\nagain after a station or machine restart and a run is never repeated, and one missed while the machine was off or\nasleep runs once when it is back within the same period (the same day for a daily one); older ones are not made up.\nCreated after today's time has passed, a daily schedule would run at once: when the person did not ask for a run now,\nwrite the current slot to `.last` before starting the job, so the first run is the next one.\n\nTime zone: the person's, set with `TZ` (a person writing Chinese is most likely on Asia/Shanghai; the station's own\nzone may differ). Name it in the confirmation so they can correct it.\n\n## 2. What a run does\n\n- **A command** (a backup, a sync, fetching a file): run it in the loop in place of the notify line, and tell only\n  what matters: `out=$(<command> 2>&1) || stillfail-job notify \"daily-backup $slot failed: $(printf '%s' \"$out\" | tail -5)\"`.\n  Nothing wakes you on success unless the person wants to hear of each run.\n- **Work for you** (a report, a summary, checking something and saying what changed, a reminder): the notify wakes\n  you. Make its words a complete instruction: the chat may have been compacted or be in a new session by then, so\n  name what to do, where to post (the thread address), and anything to compare against (a file in the workspace).\n\n## 3. Start it and confirm\n\n- `job_start` with `command: \"sh cron/<name>.sh\"`, `name` (e.g. `定时 · 每天 9:00 日报`) and `watch: true`: the chat is\n  then listed under 监控中, is never archived for being idle, and is asked about before archiving by hand (archiving\n  stops its jobs, and the schedule with them).\n- Post once in the chat: what runs, the schedule with its time zone, when the next run is, and that telling you in\n  this chat changes or stops it. Give the post a title naming the schedule (e.g. `定时 · 每天 9:00 日报`). End the\n  turn `waiting` (for: the schedule): with the watch running, only its notify, its end or a message wakes you.\n- On each run: do the work, post the result in the chat it is for, and end `waiting` again. A failed run: say so\n  there, fix the script if it is the cause, and restart the job.\n\n## Changing, stopping, several\n\n- `job_list` shows the schedules running; change one by editing its script, `job_stop` and `job_start` again (the\n  `.last` file carries over). Stop it with `job_stop` when asked, then end the chat normally.\n- Several schedules in one chat are several jobs (at most 20 jobs per chat).\n- A schedule runs on this station, and only while its machine is on: a laptop that sleeps runs a missed run when it\n  wakes. If it must run on time on another machine, the run can wake you and you use `station_task` there; or open the\n  chat on that station.","about":"Run something on a schedule or at a set time (每天 9 点、每周一、每 30 分钟、明天提醒我, cron, a daily report, a reminder, a periodic check) with a still.fail job. Read it before setting up any scheduled or recurring task, instead of crontab, launchd, systemd timers or your runtime's own scheduler.","brief":"Run something on a schedule or at a set time with a still.fail job. Read it before setting up any scheduled or recurring task, instead of crontab, launchd, systemd timers or your runtime's own scheduler.","size":"5.0 KB"},{"name":"stillfail-feedback","description":"Offer to report bugs in still.fail apps, station tools or message delivery to its team. Excludes user code, other services and agent mistakes; send only after approval.","project":false,"builtin":true,"text":"---\nname: stillfail-feedback\ndescription: Offer to report bugs in still.fail apps, station tools or message delivery to its team. Excludes user code, other services and agent mistakes; send only after approval.\n---\n\n# Report a still.fail bug\n\nUse for broken station tools, missing/duplicated/misrouted messages, unexpected session restarts or job failures,\nand problems with still.fail apps, notifications or sign-in. Exclude user projects, external outages, model\nmistakes, invalid tool arguments and stated limits (allowances, file size). If unsure, inspect the error or retry\nonce before attributing it to still.fail.\n\n## Offer\n\nHandle the user's task first, working around the issue if possible. Offer once per problem, in their language:\n“这看起来是 still.fail 本身的问题，要不要我帮你反馈给开发团队？” If declined or unanswered, drop it.\nDo not offer for transient hiccups resolved on retry.\n\n## Prepare and confirm\n\nWrite a report the team can understand without the conversation, in its language:\n- title: what goes wrong, one line.\n- body (Markdown): actual/expected behaviour, exact error, reproduction steps, time/frequency and relevant tool names/ids.\n- area: station, web, android, desktop, slack, cloud or unknown.\n- reporter: person's name and Slack channel or “still.fail chat”.\n- logs: only the few relevant tool errors or station output lines.\n\nStation version, runtime/model, session, thread and its link are added automatically; do not repeat them.\nExclude conversation text, files, code, customer names and secrets (tokens, keys, passwords, private URLs) by\ndefault. If a short excerpt is essential, include only that and point it out when showing the report.\n\nShow the exact title, body and logs, then ask for confirmation. Revise and show again if requested. Only after\napproval call `feedback_send` with this thread as `to`. Tell the user its FB number and that a fix will be reported\nhere. When the station announces that fix, relay it briefly in the same thread.\n\nIf sending fails, say so and provide the report for the user to pass on.\n","files":[],"share":null,"body":"# Report a still.fail bug\n\nUse for broken station tools, missing/duplicated/misrouted messages, unexpected session restarts or job failures,\nand problems with still.fail apps, notifications or sign-in. Exclude user projects, external outages, model\nmistakes, invalid tool arguments and stated limits (allowances, file size). If unsure, inspect the error or retry\nonce before attributing it to still.fail.\n\n## Offer\n\nHandle the user's task first, working around the issue if possible. Offer once per problem, in their language:\n“这看起来是 still.fail 本身的问题，要不要我帮你反馈给开发团队？” If declined or unanswered, drop it.\nDo not offer for transient hiccups resolved on retry.\n\n## Prepare and confirm\n\nWrite a report the team can understand without the conversation, in its language:\n- title: what goes wrong, one line.\n- body (Markdown): actual/expected behaviour, exact error, reproduction steps, time/frequency and relevant tool names/ids.\n- area: station, web, android, desktop, slack, cloud or unknown.\n- reporter: person's name and Slack channel or “still.fail chat”.\n- logs: only the few relevant tool errors or station output lines.\n\nStation version, runtime/model, session, thread and its link are added automatically; do not repeat them.\nExclude conversation text, files, code, customer names and secrets (tokens, keys, passwords, private URLs) by\ndefault. If a short excerpt is essential, include only that and point it out when showing the report.\n\nShow the exact title, body and logs, then ask for confirmation. Revise and show again if requested. Only after\napproval call `feedback_send` with this thread as `to`. Tell the user its FB number and that a fix will be reported\nhere. When the station announces that fix, relay it briefly in the same thread.\n\nIf sending fails, say so and provide the report for the user to pass on.","about":"Offer to report bugs in still.fail apps, station tools or message delivery to its team. Excludes user code, other services and agent mistakes; send only after approval.","brief":"Offer to report bugs in still.fail apps, station tools or message delivery to its team. Excludes user code, other services and agent mistakes; send only after approval.","size":"1.8 KB"},{"name":"stillfail-jobs","description":"Run background jobs, long-running watches, web previews or tasks on another still.fail station, or reach a service on another station (its HTTP API by URL, or any port forwarded here). Use for work that outlives a turn, a browser service people need to use, or a service (an LLM server, an API, a dev server, a database) another station runs.","project":false,"builtin":true,"text":"---\nname: stillfail-jobs\ndescription: Run background jobs, long-running watches, web previews or tasks on another still.fail station, or reach a service on another station (its HTTP API by URL, or any port forwarded here). Use for work that outlives a turn, a browser service people need to use, or a service (an LLM server, an API, a dev server, a database) another station runs.\n---\n\n# Jobs and services\n\nJobs run in separate process groups with persistent logs. They survive turns and station restarts; interrupted jobs\nrestart. Completion and `stillfail-job notify` messages wake you. A job with a port is a service, kept running and\navailable to workspace members through its returned link.\n\n## Background work\n\n- Use `job_start` without a port for long builds, tests, data work or loops. Run quick commands directly; if a result\n  is needed before answering, wait for it in the turn.\n- Supply `command` (run by `sh -c`), a short `name`, and optionally `cwd` (defaults to the session workspace).\n  Keep clones and outputs in that workspace.\n- Tell people what is running, carry on with whatever does not depend on it, and end `waiting` only when nothing\n  else is left; use `need_human` instead if you also need their input.\n- To wait on CI or another remote run, start a job (or background command) that polls it and exits on the first\n  failure: its completion wakes you. Do not end `waiting` on a guessed duration with nothing watching.\n- Inside long jobs, `stillfail-job notify \"<words>\"` reports meaningful milestones or trouble, not every line.\n  `ember-job` is an alias. Notices arrive via=\"ember\"; relay only what matters and act on failures.\n- Completion includes the exit code and log tail. `job_log` reads more (up to 1000 lines); `job_list` shows your\n  jobs; `job_stop` stops one. A completed step needs no separate user post unless it is useful news.\n\n## Watches\n\nFor an explicitly requested ongoing watch (CI, deployment, metrics, review queue) that outlives your own work, set `watch: true` and loop,\nnotifying on changes. The chat appears under 监控中, does not auto-archive, and asks before manual archiving.\nEnd `waiting`: its timeout will not wake you while the watch runs; a notice, completion or user message will.\nGive the next post a title naming the watch (e.g. 监控 · PR #482 的 CI); watch chats can be renamed immediately.\nStop the job when no longer needed; the chat then becomes ordinary again.\n\n## Web services\n\nUse a port for something people should try in a browser: a UI, prototype, dashboard or tool. A one-off answer,\nstatic image or internal-only background task does not need a service.\n\n1. Start with `command`, `name`, and `port` (1024–65535). Listen on `$PORT`; 127.0.0.1 is enough, e.g.\n   `python3 -m http.server $PORT`. One service per port.\n2. Verify it answers with `curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:<port>/`; retry briefly or inspect `job_log` on failure.\n3. Post the returned `link` where requested (Slack: `<link|label>`), naming the service and what to try, not its\n   port. It is private to workspace members, not a public URL.\n4. Stop unused services with `job_stop`. To restart, stop then start (or let a dev server reload itself).\n   Repeated crashes cause restarts with increasing delays and notices: inspect logs and fix the cause.\n\nServices open in a frame on their own origin. Use root paths (`/`). Responses are relayed whole: WebSockets,\nSSE and long-poll streams do not pass through. Disable browser hot reload; live data only appears when read.\nKeep the shown version running during review; compare another version via a separate checkout/service.\nThe preview supports phone/tablet/desktop sizes, rotation, zoom and screenshot annotations; tell reviewers they\ncan mark places and comment. Use stillfail-show for evidence to accompany the link.\n\n## Another station\n\nUse `station_list` to see the workspace's stations and how busy each is right now (cores, arch/OS, GPU, CPU busy over\nthe last minute, load average, free memory and disk where tasks run, what it is running); `station_list {station}`\ngives one in detail with its task capabilities. Any station of the workspace accepts tasks from here; one not updated\nyet may still refuse them, and an older one's load shows as unknown.\n\nChoosing where to run: the right OS and architecture first; then enough free memory and disk for the job (a large\nbuild needs several GiB of each); then the least busy (low CPU busy and load per core, few running tasks). Heavy\nbuilds and test suites go to a big machine, not a small VM. Offline stations are skipped. There are no locks: do\nnot wait for, ask for or announce a \"lock\" on a machine, and do not queue behind other agents' claims. Look at the\nload, and if a machine is busy pick another or run anyway when nothing better is free.\n\nPrepare `station_task` with a stable `key`, upload inputs with `station_file`, then start the same key. Commands\nrun as the target OS user, not a sandbox. All tasks of this session share one directory on the target (the working\ndirectory of each task), so a later task can build in what an earlier one cloned. Keep checkouts, worktrees and build\noutput inside it rather than elsewhere on that machine (or over ssh): it is removed with anything still running when\nthis chat is archived or deleted, or after 14 days unused, which is what keeps the target's disk free. Pin inputs and\nversions: clone or fetch the intended revision there, or upload a bundle.\n\nTo build or test a local working directory there (a checkout with uncommitted changes), prepare with `dir` (its\nabsolute path here) instead of uploading: each start syncs it into a mirror on the target (named in prepare's answer,\ninside the session directory) and runs the command there. Only files Git tracks or would add (all but the usual build\nand dependency directories outside Git) go, and only what changed; the target's build output and dependencies stay\nwarm. `back: [paths]` downloads outputs into `station-back/<key>/` here when the task ends. Synced files the command\nchanged come back as `station-back/<key>/changes.patch`: review it and `git apply` it in the directory if wanted. One task\nat a time per mirror.\n\n`station_task` lists, inspects, tails or stops your remote tasks. Completion and notices survive reconnects and\nstation restarts; no local polling job is needed. End `waiting`, naming the task. Download artifacts with\n`station_file` and post them to the requesting chat. Downloads create new files, never overwrite; inputs may be\nuploaded again before start.\n\nAfter a timeout, inspect or retry the same key: work may already have run. Lost remote processes are not restarted\nautomatically; `failed` without an exit code means the outcome may be uncertain. Inspect before using a new key.\n\n## Another station's services\n\nServices listening on another station's localhost are reachable from here with no port opened anywhere, two ways.\n`<station>` is its name from `station_list` (any case) or, when a name is not unique, its id.\n\n- **A program calling an HTTP API with a base URL** (an LLM server, a REST API, a script): use the path form,\n  `$STILLFAIL_PEER_HTTP/<station>/<port>/<path>` = `http://localhost:<port>/<path>` there (streams, large bodies and\n  WebSockets pass as they come), e.g. `OLLAMA_HOST=$STILLFAIL_PEER_HTTP/studio/11434`. Without the variable, it is\n  your stillfail MCP URL with `/mcp` replaced by `/peer`. Programs only (Host 127.0.0.1, no browser `Origin`). 502\n  \"needs an update\": that station is from before this; 504: it is offline.\n- **Anything else: a browser, web pages, dev servers (HMR), databases, any TCP protocol**: forward the port with\n  `peer_forward {station, port}`, like `ssh -L`. It listens on 127.0.0.1 here, at the same port when free (the answer\n  gives the address and URL), and passes bytes as they are, so absolute paths, client routing, redirects, cookies and\n  WebSockets work as on that machine, and `psql -h 127.0.0.1 -p <port>`, `redis-cli` and the like too. It closes\n  when this chat is archived, when the `job` you tie it to ends, after 30 minutes with no connection, or when this\n  station restarts (then open it again). `peer_forward {}` lists yours and whether each station is online;\n  `peer_forward {close: <local port>}` closes one. A path prefix breaks web pages, so do not use the path form for them.\n- To drive another station's Chrome over CDP, its DevTools WebSocket works through either: forward its debugging\n  port and use `ws://127.0.0.1:<port>/devtools/...` as `/json/version` gives it, or take that URL's path under\n  `$STILLFAIL_PEER_HTTP/<station>/<port>` (with `ws://`).\n- For people's browsers (a link to post), start a job with a port instead: forwards are this machine's only.\n","files":[],"share":null,"body":"# Jobs and services\n\nJobs run in separate process groups with persistent logs. They survive turns and station restarts; interrupted jobs\nrestart. Completion and `stillfail-job notify` messages wake you. A job with a port is a service, kept running and\navailable to workspace members through its returned link.\n\n## Background work\n\n- Use `job_start` without a port for long builds, tests, data work or loops. Run quick commands directly; if a result\n  is needed before answering, wait for it in the turn.\n- Supply `command` (run by `sh -c`), a short `name`, and optionally `cwd` (defaults to the session workspace).\n  Keep clones and outputs in that workspace.\n- Tell people what is running, carry on with whatever does not depend on it, and end `waiting` only when nothing\n  else is left; use `need_human` instead if you also need their input.\n- To wait on CI or another remote run, start a job (or background command) that polls it and exits on the first\n  failure: its completion wakes you. Do not end `waiting` on a guessed duration with nothing watching.\n- Inside long jobs, `stillfail-job notify \"<words>\"` reports meaningful milestones or trouble, not every line.\n  `ember-job` is an alias. Notices arrive via=\"ember\"; relay only what matters and act on failures.\n- Completion includes the exit code and log tail. `job_log` reads more (up to 1000 lines); `job_list` shows your\n  jobs; `job_stop` stops one. A completed step needs no separate user post unless it is useful news.\n\n## Watches\n\nFor an explicitly requested ongoing watch (CI, deployment, metrics, review queue) that outlives your own work, set `watch: true` and loop,\nnotifying on changes. The chat appears under 监控中, does not auto-archive, and asks before manual archiving.\nEnd `waiting`: its timeout will not wake you while the watch runs; a notice, completion or user message will.\nGive the next post a title naming the watch (e.g. 监控 · PR #482 的 CI); watch chats can be renamed immediately.\nStop the job when no longer needed; the chat then becomes ordinary again.\n\n## Web services\n\nUse a port for something people should try in a browser: a UI, prototype, dashboard or tool. A one-off answer,\nstatic image or internal-only background task does not need a service.\n\n1. Start with `command`, `name`, and `port` (1024–65535). Listen on `$PORT`; 127.0.0.1 is enough, e.g.\n   `python3 -m http.server $PORT`. One service per port.\n2. Verify it answers with `curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:<port>/`; retry briefly or inspect `job_log` on failure.\n3. Post the returned `link` where requested (Slack: `<link|label>`), naming the service and what to try, not its\n   port. It is private to workspace members, not a public URL.\n4. Stop unused services with `job_stop`. To restart, stop then start (or let a dev server reload itself).\n   Repeated crashes cause restarts with increasing delays and notices: inspect logs and fix the cause.\n\nServices open in a frame on their own origin. Use root paths (`/`). Responses are relayed whole: WebSockets,\nSSE and long-poll streams do not pass through. Disable browser hot reload; live data only appears when read.\nKeep the shown version running during review; compare another version via a separate checkout/service.\nThe preview supports phone/tablet/desktop sizes, rotation, zoom and screenshot annotations; tell reviewers they\ncan mark places and comment. Use stillfail-show for evidence to accompany the link.\n\n## Another station\n\nUse `station_list` to see the workspace's stations and how busy each is right now (cores, arch/OS, GPU, CPU busy over\nthe last minute, load average, free memory and disk where tasks run, what it is running); `station_list {station}`\ngives one in detail with its task capabilities. Any station of the workspace accepts tasks from here; one not updated\nyet may still refuse them, and an older one's load shows as unknown.\n\nChoosing where to run: the right OS and architecture first; then enough free memory and disk for the job (a large\nbuild needs several GiB of each); then the least busy (low CPU busy and load per core, few running tasks). Heavy\nbuilds and test suites go to a big machine, not a small VM. Offline stations are skipped. There are no locks: do\nnot wait for, ask for or announce a \"lock\" on a machine, and do not queue behind other agents' claims. Look at the\nload, and if a machine is busy pick another or run anyway when nothing better is free.\n\nPrepare `station_task` with a stable `key`, upload inputs with `station_file`, then start the same key. Commands\nrun as the target OS user, not a sandbox. All tasks of this session share one directory on the target (the working\ndirectory of each task), so a later task can build in what an earlier one cloned. Keep checkouts, worktrees and build\noutput inside it rather than elsewhere on that machine (or over ssh): it is removed with anything still running when\nthis chat is archived or deleted, or after 14 days unused, which is what keeps the target's disk free. Pin inputs and\nversions: clone or fetch the intended revision there, or upload a bundle.\n\nTo build or test a local working directory there (a checkout with uncommitted changes), prepare with `dir` (its\nabsolute path here) instead of uploading: each start syncs it into a mirror on the target (named in prepare's answer,\ninside the session directory) and runs the command there. Only files Git tracks or would add (all but the usual build\nand dependency directories outside Git) go, and only what changed; the target's build output and dependencies stay\nwarm. `back: [paths]` downloads outputs into `station-back/<key>/` here when the task ends. Synced files the command\nchanged come back as `station-back/<key>/changes.patch`: review it and `git apply` it in the directory if wanted. One task\nat a time per mirror.\n\n`station_task` lists, inspects, tails or stops your remote tasks. Completion and notices survive reconnects and\nstation restarts; no local polling job is needed. End `waiting`, naming the task. Download artifacts with\n`station_file` and post them to the requesting chat. Downloads create new files, never overwrite; inputs may be\nuploaded again before start.\n\nAfter a timeout, inspect or retry the same key: work may already have run. Lost remote processes are not restarted\nautomatically; `failed` without an exit code means the outcome may be uncertain. Inspect before using a new key.\n\n## Another station's services\n\nServices listening on another station's localhost are reachable from here with no port opened anywhere, two ways.\n`<station>` is its name from `station_list` (any case) or, when a name is not unique, its id.\n\n- **A program calling an HTTP API with a base URL** (an LLM server, a REST API, a script): use the path form,\n  `$STILLFAIL_PEER_HTTP/<station>/<port>/<path>` = `http://localhost:<port>/<path>` there (streams, large bodies and\n  WebSockets pass as they come), e.g. `OLLAMA_HOST=$STILLFAIL_PEER_HTTP/studio/11434`. Without the variable, it is\n  your stillfail MCP URL with `/mcp` replaced by `/peer`. Programs only (Host 127.0.0.1, no browser `Origin`). 502\n  \"needs an update\": that station is from before this; 504: it is offline.\n- **Anything else: a browser, web pages, dev servers (HMR), databases, any TCP protocol**: forward the port with\n  `peer_forward {station, port}`, like `ssh -L`. It listens on 127.0.0.1 here, at the same port when free (the answer\n  gives the address and URL), and passes bytes as they are, so absolute paths, client routing, redirects, cookies and\n  WebSockets work as on that machine, and `psql -h 127.0.0.1 -p <port>`, `redis-cli` and the like too. It closes\n  when this chat is archived, when the `job` you tie it to ends, after 30 minutes with no connection, or when this\n  station restarts (then open it again). `peer_forward {}` lists yours and whether each station is online;\n  `peer_forward {close: <local port>}` closes one. A path prefix breaks web pages, so do not use the path form for them.\n- To drive another station's Chrome over CDP, its DevTools WebSocket works through either: forward its debugging\n  port and use `ws://127.0.0.1:<port>/devtools/...` as `/json/version` gives it, or take that URL's path under\n  `$STILLFAIL_PEER_HTTP/<station>/<port>` (with `ws://`).\n- For people's browsers (a link to post), start a job with a port instead: forwards are this machine's only.","about":"Run background jobs, long-running watches, web previews or tasks on another still.fail station, or reach a service on another station (its HTTP API by URL, or any port forwarded here). Use for work that outlives a turn, a browser service people need to use, or a service (an LLM server, an API, a dev server, a database) another station runs.","brief":"Run background jobs, long-running watches, web previews or tasks on another still.fail station, or reach a service on another station. Use for work that outlives a turn, a browser service people need to use, or a service another station runs.","size":"8.2 KB"},{"name":"stillfail-login","description":"Which login command to give the login tool for common CLIs (gh, gcloud, aws, az, npm, docker, vercel, wrangler, firebase) so the person can finish it from the login card, and which flags lead to steps the card cannot take.","project":false,"builtin":true,"text":"---\nname: stillfail-login\ndescription: Which login command to give the login tool for common CLIs (gh, gcloud, aws, az, npm, docker, vercel, wrangler, firebase) so the person can finish it from the login card, and which flags lead to steps the card cannot take.\n---\n\n# Logging this station in to a CLI\n\nThe `login` tool runs the tool's own login command here; the person does only the browser step, from the login card in\ntheir still.fail app. What the card can take, by what the command does:\n\n- **Device code** (a page and a code): every client, the web and phones included. Prefer it where the tool has one.\n- **Page with a code to paste back** (the command asks for \"the verification code\" after printing a page): every\n  client; the person pastes it on the card. Do not answer that prompt yourself.\n- **Loopback** (the page comes back to `localhost:<port>` here): the desktop and Android apps only; a person on the\n  web cannot finish it.\n- **A page to open** (the command polls by itself): every client.\n- **A password or token typed in the terminal**: nobody; it is refused. Use another way, or say it cannot be done here.\n\nWhich command, as tested (docs/remote-login.md):\n\n| Tool | Use | Flow | Avoid |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| gh | `gh auth login --web -h github.com --insecure-storage` | device code (answer its Y/n yourself) | plain `gh auth login` (menus, a token option); keychain storage |\n| gcloud (CLI) | `gcloud auth login` | loopback, port 8085 | `--no-browser` (needs gcloud on another machine) |\n| gcloud, person on the web | `gcloud auth login --no-launch-browser` | page, then the code pasted on the card | – |\n| gcloud (SDK/ADC) | `gcloud auth application-default login`, only when code needs ADC or a person asks | loopback | doing it in place of `gcloud auth login`: they are separate credentials |\n| aws | `aws sso login --use-device-code` | device code | – |\n| az | `az login --use-device-code` | device code; may then ask for a subscription (answer it) | – |\n| npm | `npm login` | a page to open | `--auth-type=legacy` (username and password) |\n| docker | `docker login` | device code (Docker Hub) | – |\n| vercel | `vercel login` | device code | – |\n| wrangler | `wrangler login --device` | device code (8 characters, 5 minutes) | plain `wrangler login` (loopback, fails if 8976 is taken) |\n| firebase | `firebase login` | loopback; first asks two Y/n questions (answer them) | – |\n\nBefore starting:\n\n- Check whether it is already logged in (`gh auth status`, `gcloud auth list`, `aws sts get-caller-identity`,\n  `az account show`, `npm whoami`…). Say who it is logged in as; start a login only if another account is wanted or it\n  has expired.\n- A tool not listed: take its device-code option if it has one (`--device`, `--device-code`, `--use-device-code`),\n  else its default browser login.\n- The agents' own runtimes (Claude Code, Codex) are signed in from the station's accounts page, not with this tool.\n\nAfter it ends, check it worked (the status command again) and say which account and project or org it is on.\n","files":[],"share":null,"body":"# Logging this station in to a CLI\n\nThe `login` tool runs the tool's own login command here; the person does only the browser step, from the login card in\ntheir still.fail app. What the card can take, by what the command does:\n\n- **Device code** (a page and a code): every client, the web and phones included. Prefer it where the tool has one.\n- **Page with a code to paste back** (the command asks for \"the verification code\" after printing a page): every\n  client; the person pastes it on the card. Do not answer that prompt yourself.\n- **Loopback** (the page comes back to `localhost:<port>` here): the desktop and Android apps only; a person on the\n  web cannot finish it.\n- **A page to open** (the command polls by itself): every client.\n- **A password or token typed in the terminal**: nobody; it is refused. Use another way, or say it cannot be done here.\n\nWhich command, as tested (docs/remote-login.md):\n\n| Tool | Use | Flow | Avoid |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| gh | `gh auth login --web -h github.com --insecure-storage` | device code (answer its Y/n yourself) | plain `gh auth login` (menus, a token option); keychain storage |\n| gcloud (CLI) | `gcloud auth login` | loopback, port 8085 | `--no-browser` (needs gcloud on another machine) |\n| gcloud, person on the web | `gcloud auth login --no-launch-browser` | page, then the code pasted on the card | – |\n| gcloud (SDK/ADC) | `gcloud auth application-default login`, only when code needs ADC or a person asks | loopback | doing it in place of `gcloud auth login`: they are separate credentials |\n| aws | `aws sso login --use-device-code` | device code | – |\n| az | `az login --use-device-code` | device code; may then ask for a subscription (answer it) | – |\n| npm | `npm login` | a page to open | `--auth-type=legacy` (username and password) |\n| docker | `docker login` | device code (Docker Hub) | – |\n| vercel | `vercel login` | device code | – |\n| wrangler | `wrangler login --device` | device code (8 characters, 5 minutes) | plain `wrangler login` (loopback, fails if 8976 is taken) |\n| firebase | `firebase login` | loopback; first asks two Y/n questions (answer them) | – |\n\nBefore starting:\n\n- Check whether it is already logged in (`gh auth status`, `gcloud auth list`, `aws sts get-caller-identity`,\n  `az account show`, `npm whoami`…). Say who it is logged in as; start a login only if another account is wanted or it\n  has expired.\n- A tool not listed: take its device-code option if it has one (`--device`, `--device-code`, `--use-device-code`),\n  else its default browser login.\n- The agents' own runtimes (Claude Code, Codex) are signed in from the station's accounts page, not with this tool.\n\nAfter it ends, check it worked (the status command again) and say which account and project or org it is on.","about":"Which login command to give the login tool for common CLIs (gh, gcloud, aws, az, npm, docker, vercel, wrangler, firebase) so the person can finish it from the login card, and which flags lead to steps the card cannot take.","brief":"Which login command to give the login tool for common CLIs so the person can finish it from the login card, and which flags lead to steps the card cannot take.","size":"2.7 KB"},{"name":"stillfail-show","description":"Show evidence of visible work such as a UI, animation or generated image. Use when people need to judge appearance or interaction.","project":false,"builtin":true,"text":"---\nname: stillfail-show\ndescription: Show evidence of visible work such as a UI, animation or generated image. Use when people need to judge appearance or interaction.\n---\n\n# Show visible work\n\nShow the result, not just a description or a request that the user check it themselves. Say what changed, where\nthe evidence came from and what to look at.\n\n| To judge | Show |\n|---|---|\n| Layout, spacing, colours, a state | Images, compared at a glance |\n| Animation, transitions, a sequence | Short video; a frame strip for detailed study |\n| Something to click, type in or resize | Web service via stillfail-jobs |\n| Data, diagrams, comparisons or choices | Inline HTML via stillfail-viz |\n\nImages plus a service often work together: quick visual review and a working version to try.\n\n## Images\n\n- In still.fail, attach with `chat_post(files=[…])` and place with `![](after.png)`. Images on one line appear\n  side by side at the same height: `![](before.png) ![](after.png)`.\n- Images display at most 360×300, opening full size on click. Crop to the changed area; include a full view when\n  context matters. Use device scale 2 for sharpness.\n- Use distinct filenames per post. PNG for UI/text; JPEG for photos or size constraints. Limit: 10 files, 50 MB each.\n- Slack uploads below the text; omit Markdown image syntax and describe what the evidence shows.\n\n## Video\n\n- Place like an image: `![](demo.webm)` shows a still with a play button. Use mp4 (H.264) or webm (VP8/VP9).\n  Keep it short, crop to the motion and say what to watch.\n- Browser recordings (Playwright/CDP) can drop frames on slow machines. Record and screenshot in separate runs\n  to avoid blank frames. For exact motion, pause `document.getAnimations()`, step `currentTime` by 1000/60 ms,\n  capture each step and assemble a video or contact sheet. Real-time-dependent motion can instead be slowed with\n  CDP `Animation.setPlaybackRate` and recorded live.\n\n## Services and inline HTML\n\n- Use stillfail-jobs to start, verify, share and keep a service available for review.\n- Use stillfail-viz for inline charts, diagrams and option widgets (`ember.sendFollowUpMessage` can put a choice\n  in the user's composer). Inline HTML runs in still.fail's sandbox/style; it is not evidence of your actual UI.\n  Screenshot or serve that UI instead.\n- Full-screen pages (promo, player, slideshow) work better as an unplaced attachment or service. Include a\n  screenshot/video in the chat so the result is visible immediately.\n\n## A person on the station's screen\n\nWhen a person has to act on this machine's display or a browser tab of the station (log in, solve a captcha,\napprove a prompt, judge something live), the `screen` tool gives the link that opens it in their still.fail app,\nwhere they see and drive it. Post the link with `chat_post kind=need_human` saying what to do there, and end the\nturn; `screen` later says whether someone is viewing or controlling it. Not for evidence: show that as above.\nThe `computer` tool lets you see and operate a screen yourself (screenshots, clicks, keys); for web pages prefer the\nbrowser's DevTools (stillfail-browser), and when you are stuck hand the screen to a person with its link.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Capture the actual build/page with realistic data, intended sizes and light/dark themes. Label mock-ups as proposals.\n- Compare before/after at the same size, data and scroll position. Design alternatives should differ in structure,\n  not just a few pixels.\n- Inspect images and motion frames yourself: check the change is visible, the build/cache is current, the server\n  belongs to you, and nothing else broke. Measured values alone do not demonstrate motion.\n- Name the environment and branch/commit. State anything not shown, why, and how it can be checked later.\n- Send fresh evidence after each revision.\n","files":[],"share":null,"body":"# Show visible work\n\nShow the result, not just a description or a request that the user check it themselves. Say what changed, where\nthe evidence came from and what to look at.\n\n| To judge | Show |\n|---|---|\n| Layout, spacing, colours, a state | Images, compared at a glance |\n| Animation, transitions, a sequence | Short video; a frame strip for detailed study |\n| Something to click, type in or resize | Web service via stillfail-jobs |\n| Data, diagrams, comparisons or choices | Inline HTML via stillfail-viz |\n\nImages plus a service often work together: quick visual review and a working version to try.\n\n## Images\n\n- In still.fail, attach with `chat_post(files=[…])` and place with `![](after.png)`. Images on one line appear\n  side by side at the same height: `![](before.png) ![](after.png)`.\n- Images display at most 360×300, opening full size on click. Crop to the changed area; include a full view when\n  context matters. Use device scale 2 for sharpness.\n- Use distinct filenames per post. PNG for UI/text; JPEG for photos or size constraints. Limit: 10 files, 50 MB each.\n- Slack uploads below the text; omit Markdown image syntax and describe what the evidence shows.\n\n## Video\n\n- Place like an image: `![](demo.webm)` shows a still with a play button. Use mp4 (H.264) or webm (VP8/VP9).\n  Keep it short, crop to the motion and say what to watch.\n- Browser recordings (Playwright/CDP) can drop frames on slow machines. Record and screenshot in separate runs\n  to avoid blank frames. For exact motion, pause `document.getAnimations()`, step `currentTime` by 1000/60 ms,\n  capture each step and assemble a video or contact sheet. Real-time-dependent motion can instead be slowed with\n  CDP `Animation.setPlaybackRate` and recorded live.\n\n## Services and inline HTML\n\n- Use stillfail-jobs to start, verify, share and keep a service available for review.\n- Use stillfail-viz for inline charts, diagrams and option widgets (`ember.sendFollowUpMessage` can put a choice\n  in the user's composer). Inline HTML runs in still.fail's sandbox/style; it is not evidence of your actual UI.\n  Screenshot or serve that UI instead.\n- Full-screen pages (promo, player, slideshow) work better as an unplaced attachment or service. Include a\n  screenshot/video in the chat so the result is visible immediately.\n\n## A person on the station's screen\n\nWhen a person has to act on this machine's display or a browser tab of the station (log in, solve a captcha,\napprove a prompt, judge something live), the `screen` tool gives the link that opens it in their still.fail app,\nwhere they see and drive it. Post the link with `chat_post kind=need_human` saying what to do there, and end the\nturn; `screen` later says whether someone is viewing or controlling it. Not for evidence: show that as above.\nThe `computer` tool lets you see and operate a screen yourself (screenshots, clicks, keys); for web pages prefer the\nbrowser's DevTools (stillfail-browser), and when you are stuck hand the screen to a person with its link.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Capture the actual build/page with realistic data, intended sizes and light/dark themes. Label mock-ups as proposals.\n- Compare before/after at the same size, data and scroll position. Design alternatives should differ in structure,\n  not just a few pixels.\n- Inspect images and motion frames yourself: check the change is visible, the build/cache is current, the server\n  belongs to you, and nothing else broke. Measured values alone do not demonstrate motion.\n- Name the environment and branch/commit. State anything not shown, why, and how it can be checked later.\n- Send fresh evidence after each revision.","about":"Show evidence of visible work such as a UI, animation or generated image. Use when people need to judge appearance or interaction.","brief":"Show evidence of visible work such as a UI, animation or generated image. Use when people need to judge appearance or interaction.","size":"3.6 KB"},{"name":"stillfail-viz","description":"Create inline HTML diagrams, charts, comparisons or interactive widgets in still.fail chats. Use when a small visual explains better than prose; Slack receives an attached file.","project":false,"builtin":true,"text":"---\nname: stillfail-viz\ndescription: Create inline HTML diagrams, charts, comparisons or interactive widgets in still.fail chats. Use when a small visual explains better than prose; Slack receives an attached file.\n---\n\n# Inline visualizations\n\nIn still.fail (EMBER/…), placed HTML attachments render in the message. An HTML code fence is only code;\nan unplaced attachment opens as a file. For a simple flow, sequence, state machine or timeline, use a Mermaid\ncode fence instead: it renders in still.fail colours without a file.\n\n## Post\n\nWrite an HTML fragment in the session workspace, e.g. `session-lifecycle.html`. Attach with\n`chat_post(files=[\"/abs/path/session-lifecycle.html\"])` and put `[Session lifecycle](session-lifecycle.html)` on\nits own line in the text. Explain it around the link: figures are not read aloud and phone apps may show only files.\nIn Slack, attach but omit the Markdown link; files upload below the text and also render in still.fail's copy.\nSay what the figure shows for people staying in Slack.\n\n## Write\n\n- Fragments omit `<html>`, `<head>` and `<body>`; order `<style>`, markup, then `<script>`. Stay well under 1 MB.\n- Use the loaded stylesheet's variables/classes below; add only layout your figure needs.\n- The sandbox has no fetch, XHR, WebSocket or access to the page, station or files. Scripts/styles may load from\n  cdnjs.cloudflare.com, esm.sh, cdn.jsdelivr.net or unpkg.com. Images must be data URIs, SVG or canvas; embed data.\n- Design for 736 px down to 320 px. Avoid fixed widths/viewport heights; wrap with `.viz-grid` / `.viz-row` and\n  `.table-responsive`. Use system `--font-sans`, weights 400–600.\n- A full document (`<!doctype>` or `<html>`) renders as written in a 16:9 window, not content-sized, without the\n  stylesheet: about 736×414 down to 320×180. All content and controls must scale together. Full-screen pages are\n  better attached unplaced or served via stillfail-jobs.\n\n## Talking back\n\n`window.ember` (the name it had before still.fail; also named `window.openai`, as Codex's Visualize has it):\n\n- `ember.widgetState` — what the widget kept last time it was shown (or null); `ember.setWidgetState(value)` keeps a\n  JSON value of up to 16 KiB on the station, restored whenever and wherever the message is shown again. Put what you\n  should know in `value.modelContent` (the person's choices, say) and the rest in `value.privateContent`:\n  `modelContent` reaches you with the next message they send, as a note beside it; keeping state never starts a turn.\n- `ember.sendFollowUpMessage({ prompt })` — puts `prompt` in the chat's composer for the person to send, as if they\n  wrote it. Only from their click or key press (a button's handler); it never sends by itself. Use it for \"ask about\n  this\", \"go with option B\", a filled-in form.\n\n## Look\n\nLight and dark follow the viewer's theme. Never hard-code colours (no `#fff`, `black`, `slate-500`): use the variables.\n\n- Surfaces and text: `--background`, `--foreground`, `--card`, `--muted`, `--muted-foreground`, `--border`,\n  `--border-strong`, `--primary` / `--primary-foreground` (ink buttons), `--accent` / `--accent-foreground` (still.fail's\n  orange, tinted ground and text), `--brand` (the orange itself), `--destructive`.\n- Status and series: `--blue`, `--green`, `--orange`, `--red`, `--purple`, `--yellow`, each with a\n  `-bg` ground (`--blue-bg`…); `--chart-1` … `--chart-6` in order for chart series. Tell series apart by label or\n  shape too. In SVG use `currentColor` or `var(--…)`; for a canvas or a library that needs real colours, read them\n  with `getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(\"--chart-1\")` and redraw on the\n  `ember-viz:theme` event.\n- Type: `--font-sans`, `--font-mono`, `--font-size-base` (14px), `--font-size-small` (12px), `.text-muted`,\n  `.text-small`, `.tabular-nums`. Nothing smaller than 11px.\n- Corners: `--radius-card`, `--radius-field`, `--radius-small`.\n\n## Classes\n\n- Layout: `.card`, `.widget` (a column with gaps), `.viz-grid` (auto columns), `.viz-row`, `.viz-controls`.\n- Figures: `.viz-stat` with `.viz-stat-label` and `.viz-stat-value`; `.viz-badge` (add `.is-blue`, `.is-green`,\n  `.is-orange`, `.is-red`, `.is-yellow`, `.is-purple`).\n- Diagrams: `.viz-flow` (add `.is-vertical`) of `.viz-node` boxes (`.viz-node-title`, `.viz-node-body`; `.is-active`\n  or a colour as above) with `.viz-arrow` between them (its text is the arrow's label); `.viz-note` for an aside.\n  For anything a flow of boxes cannot draw (cycles, curves), use inline SVG with the variables.\n- Controls: `.btn`, `.btn-primary`, `.btn-ghost`, `.btn-sm`, `.is-selected`; `.form-label`, `.form-control`,\n  `.form-select`, `.form-check` + `.form-check-input` + `.form-check-label`, `.form-switch`, `.form-range`.\n- Tabs: `.nav.nav-pills` of `.nav-link` (mark the current one `.is-selected`).\n- Tables: `.table` (cells `.num` for numbers) inside `.table-responsive`.\n- Progress: `.progress` > `.progress-bar` with `style=\"width:40%\"`.\n- Tooltips: any element with `data-tooltip=\"text\"` shows it on hover and focus.\n\nUse the classes as they are; do not restyle their sizes, borders or colours.\n\n## Example\n\n`session-lifecycle.html`, placed with `[Session lifecycle](session-lifecycle.html)`:\n\n```html\n<div class=\"widget\">\n  <div class=\"viz-flow\">\n    <div class=\"viz-node is-active\"><span class=\"viz-node-title\">hot</span><span class=\"viz-node-body\">turn running</span></div>\n    <div class=\"viz-arrow\">turn ends</div>\n    <div class=\"viz-node\"><span class=\"viz-node-title\">warm</span><span class=\"viz-node-body\">process kept</span></div>\n    <div class=\"viz-arrow\">idle 30 min</div>\n    <div class=\"viz-node\"><span class=\"viz-node-title\">cold</span><span class=\"viz-node-body\">on disk only</span></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"viz-note\">Any new message brings a session back to hot.</div>\n</div>\n```\n\nBefore posting a large or scripted one, check it renders: open the file in a browser (or a screenshot tool) and fix\nwhat does not draw or throws.\n","files":[],"share":null,"body":"# Inline visualizations\n\nIn still.fail (EMBER/…), placed HTML attachments render in the message. An HTML code fence is only code;\nan unplaced attachment opens as a file. For a simple flow, sequence, state machine or timeline, use a Mermaid\ncode fence instead: it renders in still.fail colours without a file.\n\n## Post\n\nWrite an HTML fragment in the session workspace, e.g. `session-lifecycle.html`. Attach with\n`chat_post(files=[\"/abs/path/session-lifecycle.html\"])` and put `[Session lifecycle](session-lifecycle.html)` on\nits own line in the text. Explain it around the link: figures are not read aloud and phone apps may show only files.\nIn Slack, attach but omit the Markdown link; files upload below the text and also render in still.fail's copy.\nSay what the figure shows for people staying in Slack.\n\n## Write\n\n- Fragments omit `<html>`, `<head>` and `<body>`; order `<style>`, markup, then `<script>`. Stay well under 1 MB.\n- Use the loaded stylesheet's variables/classes below; add only layout your figure needs.\n- The sandbox has no fetch, XHR, WebSocket or access to the page, station or files. Scripts/styles may load from\n  cdnjs.cloudflare.com, esm.sh, cdn.jsdelivr.net or unpkg.com. Images must be data URIs, SVG or canvas; embed data.\n- Design for 736 px down to 320 px. Avoid fixed widths/viewport heights; wrap with `.viz-grid` / `.viz-row` and\n  `.table-responsive`. Use system `--font-sans`, weights 400–600.\n- A full document (`<!doctype>` or `<html>`) renders as written in a 16:9 window, not content-sized, without the\n  stylesheet: about 736×414 down to 320×180. All content and controls must scale together. Full-screen pages are\n  better attached unplaced or served via stillfail-jobs.\n\n## Talking back\n\n`window.ember` (the name it had before still.fail; also named `window.openai`, as Codex's Visualize has it):\n\n- `ember.widgetState` — what the widget kept last time it was shown (or null); `ember.setWidgetState(value)` keeps a\n  JSON value of up to 16 KiB on the station, restored whenever and wherever the message is shown again. Put what you\n  should know in `value.modelContent` (the person's choices, say) and the rest in `value.privateContent`:\n  `modelContent` reaches you with the next message they send, as a note beside it; keeping state never starts a turn.\n- `ember.sendFollowUpMessage({ prompt })` — puts `prompt` in the chat's composer for the person to send, as if they\n  wrote it. Only from their click or key press (a button's handler); it never sends by itself. Use it for \"ask about\n  this\", \"go with option B\", a filled-in form.\n\n## Look\n\nLight and dark follow the viewer's theme. Never hard-code colours (no `#fff`, `black`, `slate-500`): use the variables.\n\n- Surfaces and text: `--background`, `--foreground`, `--card`, `--muted`, `--muted-foreground`, `--border`,\n  `--border-strong`, `--primary` / `--primary-foreground` (ink buttons), `--accent` / `--accent-foreground` (still.fail's\n  orange, tinted ground and text), `--brand` (the orange itself), `--destructive`.\n- Status and series: `--blue`, `--green`, `--orange`, `--red`, `--purple`, `--yellow`, each with a\n  `-bg` ground (`--blue-bg`…); `--chart-1` … `--chart-6` in order for chart series. Tell series apart by label or\n  shape too. In SVG use `currentColor` or `var(--…)`; for a canvas or a library that needs real colours, read them\n  with `getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(\"--chart-1\")` and redraw on the\n  `ember-viz:theme` event.\n- Type: `--font-sans`, `--font-mono`, `--font-size-base` (14px), `--font-size-small` (12px), `.text-muted`,\n  `.text-small`, `.tabular-nums`. Nothing smaller than 11px.\n- Corners: `--radius-card`, `--radius-field`, `--radius-small`.\n\n## Classes\n\n- Layout: `.card`, `.widget` (a column with gaps), `.viz-grid` (auto columns), `.viz-row`, `.viz-controls`.\n- Figures: `.viz-stat` with `.viz-stat-label` and `.viz-stat-value`; `.viz-badge` (add `.is-blue`, `.is-green`,\n  `.is-orange`, `.is-red`, `.is-yellow`, `.is-purple`).\n- Diagrams: `.viz-flow` (add `.is-vertical`) of `.viz-node` boxes (`.viz-node-title`, `.viz-node-body`; `.is-active`\n  or a colour as above) with `.viz-arrow` between them (its text is the arrow's label); `.viz-note` for an aside.\n  For anything a flow of boxes cannot draw (cycles, curves), use inline SVG with the variables.\n- Controls: `.btn`, `.btn-primary`, `.btn-ghost`, `.btn-sm`, `.is-selected`; `.form-label`, `.form-control`,\n  `.form-select`, `.form-check` + `.form-check-input` + `.form-check-label`, `.form-switch`, `.form-range`.\n- Tabs: `.nav.nav-pills` of `.nav-link` (mark the current one `.is-selected`).\n- Tables: `.table` (cells `.num` for numbers) inside `.table-responsive`.\n- Progress: `.progress` > `.progress-bar` with `style=\"width:40%\"`.\n- Tooltips: any element with `data-tooltip=\"text\"` shows it on hover and focus.\n\nUse the classes as they are; do not restyle their sizes, borders or colours.\n\n## Example\n\n`session-lifecycle.html`, placed with `[Session lifecycle](session-lifecycle.html)`:\n\n```html\n<div class=\"widget\">\n  <div class=\"viz-flow\">\n    <div class=\"viz-node is-active\"><span class=\"viz-node-title\">hot</span><span class=\"viz-node-body\">turn running</span></div>\n    <div class=\"viz-arrow\">turn ends</div>\n    <div class=\"viz-node\"><span class=\"viz-node-title\">warm</span><span class=\"viz-node-body\">process kept</span></div>\n    <div class=\"viz-arrow\">idle 30 min</div>\n    <div class=\"viz-node\"><span class=\"viz-node-title\">cold</span><span class=\"viz-node-body\">on disk only</span></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"viz-note\">Any new message brings a session back to hot.</div>\n</div>\n```\n\nBefore posting a large or scripted one, check it renders: open the file in a browser (or a screenshot tool) and fix\nwhat does not draw or throws.","about":"Create inline HTML diagrams, charts, comparisons or interactive widgets in still.fail chats. Use when a small visual explains better than prose; Slack receives an attached file.","brief":"Create inline HTML diagrams, charts, comparisons or interactive widgets in still.fail chats. Use when a small visual explains better than prose; Slack receives an attached file.","size":"5.7 KB"}]}},"w":1792456735584.444}
